From e8c8dd2d499cf6f63b398fec9670a816ca35b4c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephen Trotter <33046718+sjtrotter@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 12:04:23 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] RDP: preserve protected RDPW settings

Azure Virtual Desktop publishes protected connection files (.rdpw) whose
settings and signature the generic Remmina .rdp importer does not model.
Importing one produced a profile that could not reach the AVD gateway.

Read the protected file exactly once at import time through a descriptor
opened with O_NOFOLLOW, require a regular file of at most 1 MiB, and keep
the bytes (base64) in the profile as "rdpw_data". The generic fields are
imported from that same buffer, so the file is never re-read and a later
change to it cannot alter what was accepted. The importer also learns the
"redirectsmartcards" spelling used by AVD and maps "enablerdsaadauth:i:1"
to the "aad" security setting.

Before connecting, the buffer is filtered through an explicit allowlist of
AVD routing and authentication keys and only that filtered text is handed
to FreeRDP's native connection-file parser, which populates the live
rdpSettings. Lines starting with "/" are rejected because FreeRDP treats
them as embedded command-line arguments, and the buffer is split into lines
on CR as well as LF, exactly as that parser splits it, so a carriage return
inside a line cannot hide a setting from the filter or from the check below.
The plugin-level parse happens once, in remmina_rdp_main(), rather than for
every queued input event.

Reading, decoding, splitting, classifying and filtering a protected file
live in rdp_rdpw.c, which depends on GLib alone: the importer and the
connection path share one BOM/UTF-16 decoder and one line splitter instead
of carrying a copy each, so every one of them sees the same lines, and the
helpers can be exercised without a display or a session.

A profile is treated as Azure Virtual Desktop only when it selects the ARM
resource provider and names a gateway host. An ARM file without
"gatewayhostname" names no gateway to reach and can only fail later, so it
is refused while importing rather than stored as a profile that cannot
connect; Remmina's import API reports which file failed, and the reason is
logged. A protected file that does not select ARM is imported as an
ordinary RDP profile and keeps Remmina's own gateway transport.

Where WITH_RDP_AUTH_AAD is not defined there is no WebKit token path, so a
profile whose stored content is an AVD file is refused at connection time
with an explicit error instead of failing inside the gateway handshake.
Importing such a file still works, and the same profile connects once
Remmina is rebuilt with AAD support.

The shared MIME type gains the .rdpw and .RDPW globs so a protected file
opens with Remmina from a file manager. Both desktop entries already list
application/x-remmina in MimeType=, so the glob is all the association
needs; org.remmina.Remmina-file.desktop.in runs "remmina-file-wrapper -c",
which starts a session from the file without storing it. Import is the
persistent path: "remmina -c file.rdpw" is deliberately transient, and no
save prompt is added here.

The stored content is base64 in the .remmina file, which is no more
sensitive than the connection file it comes from but is not secret either;
storing it through Remmina's secret plugin instead is left for a later
change, as is deriving the profile from the buffer at connection time.

Tested with FreeRDP 3.30.0. The series requires FreeRDP 3.16.0 or
newer: FreeRDP_GatewayAvdScope and FreeRDP_GatewayAvdAccessAadFormat
were added in that release, and every other API used is older.

This change was prepared with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) and was
reviewed and tested by the author.
